1
00:00:00,569 --> 00:00:03,930
Picture this, your company has one cluster for dev,

2
00:00:04,210 --> 00:00:08,470
three for staging, two for production, and a couple of more.

3
00:00:08,729 --> 00:00:14,840
Now, imagine having these kind of multiple workloads and multiple clusters.

4
00:00:15,130 --> 00:00:19,590
Now having that many number of clusters, how do you manage them consistently?

5
00:00:19,959 --> 00:00:24,110
This is exactly why Google built GKE fleets.

6
00:00:24,329 --> 00:00:25,309
Let's dive in.

7
00:00:25,639 --> 00:00:30,480
A fleet is a logical grouping of Kubernetes cluster that lets you manage

8
00:00:30,480 --> 00:00:35,740
them as one unit. A fleet standardizes your identity,

9
00:00:36,080 --> 00:00:41,459
policy, configuration, services, and multi‑cluster automation.

10
00:00:41,720 --> 00:00:46,880
As per the Google definition, a fleet provides a consistent way to manage,

11
00:00:47,049 --> 00:00:52,110
secure, and observe the multiple cluster across the environments.

12
00:00:52,360 --> 00:00:57,990
Enterprises need fleet because they have multiple clusters across regions.

13
00:00:58,389 --> 00:01:00,380
They have multiple business units.

14
00:01:00,909 --> 00:01:03,509
Sometimes they have multiple environments like dev,

15
00:01:03,709 --> 00:01:08,540
stage, prod, and they have the compliance and security controls.

16
00:01:08,910 --> 00:01:11,269
Fleets solve major pain points.

17
00:01:11,550 --> 00:01:14,050
For example, if you don't have a fleet,

18
00:01:14,430 --> 00:01:17,269
then each cluster managed would be separately.

19
00:01:17,470 --> 00:01:21,970
However, along with the fleet, you can have a unified control plan.

20
00:01:22,400 --> 00:01:25,510
Without fleet, the policies are manually applied.

21
00:01:25,949 --> 00:01:30,050
However, with the fleet, you can have a global policy sync.

22
00:01:30,550 --> 00:01:33,750
No fleet causes inconsistent workloads,

23
00:01:33,949 --> 00:01:37,940
and with the fleet you can make it multi‑cluster services.

24
00:01:38,230 --> 00:01:42,779
You can also solve the problem of federated identity because fleet

25
00:01:42,779 --> 00:01:46,089
can provide you the unified workload identity.

26
00:01:46,349 --> 00:01:50,309
Fleet can also help you to resolve the drift between the clusters.

27
00:01:50,760 --> 00:01:55,139
With fleet you can have the config sync and Anthos policy controller.

28
00:01:55,480 --> 00:01:58,900
Overall, fleets solve the multi‑cluster chaos problem.

29
00:01:59,319 --> 00:02:03,500
A fleet starts with a project that acts as a fleet host,

30
00:02:03,839 --> 00:02:09,179
and then you can register GKE clusters, Anthos on‑prem clusters,

31
00:02:09,460 --> 00:02:13,839
Anthos bare metal, and you can also have multi‑cloud clusters.

32
00:02:14,710 --> 00:02:19,770
This means fleets unify cloud and on‑prem operations.

33
00:02:20,149 --> 00:02:24,029
Google Cloud recommends Workload Identity as the identity

34
00:02:24,029 --> 00:02:26,779
solution for workloads across the clusters.

35
00:02:27,029 --> 00:02:28,770
Fleets extend this model.

36
00:02:29,229 --> 00:02:30,690
One identity configuration,

37
00:02:31,070 --> 00:02:35,699
one mapping definition works across all the member clusters.

38
00:02:35,940 --> 00:02:38,270
Fleet‑wide policies and configuration.

39
00:02:38,710 --> 00:02:40,690
This is where the fleet shines.

40
00:02:40,990 --> 00:02:44,820
You point your fleet to a Git repository and fleet pulls the

41
00:02:44,820 --> 00:02:48,300
configuration from the Git and applies to every cluster.

42
00:02:48,580 --> 00:02:51,970
Here, every cluster will receive the RBAC policies,

43
00:02:52,300 --> 00:02:54,839
resource quotas, and network policies.

44
00:02:55,179 --> 00:02:57,479
It has Anthos Policy Controller.

45
00:02:57,779 --> 00:03:01,130
This enforces constraints like no privileged containers,

46
00:03:01,580 --> 00:03:05,320
no host path mounts, and only signed images allowed.

47
00:03:05,779 --> 00:03:09,750
These policies apply across all the clusters in the fleet.

48
00:03:10,229 --> 00:03:14,669
Multi‑cluster ingress creates a single global load balancer for

49
00:03:14,669 --> 00:03:17,940
workloads running across multiple clusters.

50
00:03:18,270 --> 00:03:19,770
Multi‑cluster services,

51
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:23,800
a service in a staging cluster can discover a service in a broad

52
00:03:23,800 --> 00:03:27,259
cluster, useful for multi‑region failover.

53
00:03:27,580 --> 00:03:31,750
Enterprises use this for rolling upgrades across regions.

54
00:03:32,240 --> 00:03:37,929
So that's how GKE fleets help enterprises manage cluster at scale,

55
00:03:38,270 --> 00:03:41,460
with unified identity, consistent policy,

56
00:03:41,880 --> 00:03:46,220
multi‑cluster services, and seamless hybrid cloud support.
